          "name": "What is a staggered louver facade?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A staggered louver facade uses standard louver sections, rotated individually off their own centre axis rather than sharing one uniform angle, so the resulting elevation reads as fluid and continuously shifting rather than the static, linear appearance a conventional parallel louver installation presents."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can louvers be parametric?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The specific angle for each individual louver position can be generated by a defined algorithmic rule rather than specified manually one by one, and that rule is applied consistently across the full facade, which is what makes a genuinely non-repeating, organic-reading pattern achievable using entirely standard components within a normal project budget."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why a copper finish rather than copper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Powder-coated copper on aluminium delivers the same visual warmth as genuine copper cladding, at meaningfully lower cost and weight, and it holds its colour consistently over time rather than weathering and shifting the way real copper naturally does, a genuine trade-off rather than simply a cheaper substitute."
          }
        }
